  <!-- Section 8: Common Mistakes -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Common Mistakes Brands Make with Bangladesh Manufacturers</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Most of the problems we see brands run into aren't factory problems — they're process gaps that would have caused the same issue with any overseas manufacturer. Here's what actually trips people up.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Tech Packs That Leave Too Much to Guesswork</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        A vague tech pack doesn't get filled in by common sense — it gets filled in by whatever assumption the pattern maker happens to make, which is rarely the one you had in mind. Every missing measurement, unspecified seam allowance, or "similar to reference photo" note is a coin flip on whether the sample matches what's in your head. It's genuinely cheaper to spend an extra day getting the tech pack precise than to spend two sample rounds correcting guesses.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Approving a Sample You're Not Actually Happy With</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        We see this most with first-time brands under launch-date pressure: a sample is "close enough," so it gets approved to keep things moving. Bulk production copies the approved sample exactly — including whatever you were quietly unhappy about. If something's off, that's the moment to say so, not after 500 units are cut.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Renegotiating Payment Terms Away From 30/70</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The standard structure — 30% deposit to start production, 70% before shipment — exists because it keeps both sides honest: the factory has enough capital to run production, and you keep enough leverage to insist on quality before the balance goes out. Paying more upfront, or all of it, hands that leverage away for no real benefit to you.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Taking Certifications on Faith</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        A certificate is a PDF with a number on it — check the number against the issuing body's own database before you rely on the claim, especially for organic or recycled-content claims where the certificate is doing a lot of marketing work. GOTS in particular has a public verification tool; use it rather than trusting the logo.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Planning Around Production Time, Not Total Time</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Production typically runs 40-45 days, but that's not your delivery date — add 20-40 days for shipping and customs depending on destination, more if there's a peak-season port backlog. Brands that only count production days routinely miss their own launch dates. Our <a href="/clothing-manufacturers-for-startups">clothing manufacturers for startups</a> guide walks through timeline planning in more depth if this is your first order.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Not Knowing What Your Incoterm Actually Covers</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        FOB, CIF, and DDP sound interchangeable until a customs bill lands on your desk that you didn't budget for. FOB means you're responsible for shipping once goods leave the port; CIF adds shipping but not duties; DDP covers duties and delivery too. Get this in writing before you agree to a quote, not after.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Trusting the Factory's Own Inspection Alone</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        A factory's own QC team has an obvious incentive to pass a shipment so they get paid — that doesn't make them dishonest, but it's not an independent check either. A third-party inspector costs a few hundred dollars against a container of goods that could cost tens of thousands to remake. That math isn't close.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Betting Everything on One Factory</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        A factory fire, a labor dispute, a sudden capacity crunch — any of these can stop your entire supply chain if you've got nothing else lined up. You don't need three factories running in parallel from day one, but knowing you have a qualified backup before you need one is the difference between a delay and a crisis.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 9: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Frequently Asked Questions About Bangladesh Clothing Manufacturers</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the minimum order quantity for clothing manufacturers in Bangladesh?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            It varies more than most buyers expect — anywhere from 300 to 1,000+ pieces per style depending on the factory's size and target client. SDF Clothing sets ours at 300 pieces per style with flexible size and color splits, specifically because that's the range most startup and boutique brands can actually commit to on a first order. Larger factories geared toward established brands often won't go below 2,000.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Why choose Bangladesh for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Lower labor costs, a workforce with decades of export-scale experience, and (for now) duty-free EU access under EBA all compound together. Bangladesh is the world's second-largest garment exporter, with the RMG sector shipping $39.35 billion in FY2024-25 per BGMEA. That scale means the fabric and trim supply chain a factory relies on is already mature here, which is what actually keeps lead times and pricing predictable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How long does clothing production take in Bangladesh?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bulk production typically runs 40-45 days from fabric sourcing to shipment, with sample development taking another 7-14 days before that. Add 20-40 days for shipping depending on destination — the total timeline, not just the production window, is what you should plan a launch date around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What certifications should I look for in a Bangladesh clothing manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            GOTS (organic cotton chain of custody), OEKO-TEX (chemical safety), BSCI (social compliance), WRAP (ethical manufacturing), ISO 9001 (quality management), and C-TPAT (supply chain security) cover most of what buyers ask about. SDF Clothing holds 13 certifications across these categories — check any certificate number against the issuing body's own database rather than taking a logo at face value.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What are the payment terms for Bangladesh clothing manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            30% deposit to start production, 70% before shipment, is the industry-standard split, and we'd recommend not moving far from it. We accept T/T (wire transfer), Letter of Credit, and bank transfers. Paying more upfront — or in full — removes your leverage to insist on fixes if quality issues show up before shipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How does Bangladesh's LDC graduation affect clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh graduates from Least Developed Country status in November 2026, which ends automatic EBA duty-free access to the EU. Trade negotiations for what replaces it are ongoing, and the outcome will differ by product category — if EU access matters to your sourcing plan, ask your factory directly what they're tracking on this rather than assuming the terms stay the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What services do Bangladesh clothing manufacturers offer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Full-package factories cover OEM, private label, and CMT (cut-make-trim) production alongside fabric sourcing, tech pack development, sampling, quality control, and export logistics. Not every factory offers all of these — clarify exactly what's bundled into a quote before you compare prices between suppliers, since a lower number sometimes just means fewer services included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I find a reliable clothing manufacturer in Bangladesh?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Check BGMEA or BKMEA membership, verify certifications directly with the issuing bodies, ask for recent audit reports, and call client references rather than just reading testimonials. If you can, visit the factory or ask for a video tour — a factory with nothing to hide will generally say yes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

